[stderr] ## Scope and dependency conclusions
[stderr] 
[stderr] The minimal offline campaign depends on the shell, exploration, narrative,
[stderr] quests/progression, combat, inventory, persistence, rendering/input,
[stderr] accessibility, original assets, content tooling, and quality disciplines. PWA
[stderr] packaging adds installability, caches, offline assets, and update safety; it
[stderr] does not create a separate game.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Account, multiplayer, live-service, and commerce must not become dependencies
[stderr] of the offline campaign until a human resolves their contradiction. If approved,
[stderr] they require explicit degraded states, privacy/security rules, operational
[stderr] ownership, and recovery behavior. If deferred, their interfaces and data model
[stderr] must not expand or block the first release.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Explicit requests, safe assumptions, and approval gates
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Kind | Discovery conclusion | Handling |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Explicit requests | Small 2D single-player RPG; web/PWA; exploration, town, dungeon, combat, inventory, quests, saves, settings, ending, access, feedback, recovery. | Plan as the complete offline campaign. |
[stderr] | Necessary implied capabilities | Shell/session, authored content/tooling, input/rendering, QA, release/update workflow, and balance. | Include in later contracts as enabling work, sized to the small release. |
[stderr] | Safe assumptions | Original content; shared core; durable confirmed local saves when storage permits; connected features visibly degrade; minimal-data diagnostics. | Retain as reversible guardrails only. |
[stderr] | Approval gates | Connected scope, account/sync, multiplayer/safety, commerce, stack/vendors, support matrix/budgets, content quantities, localisation, audio, and legal/store policy. | Record and obtain approval before selecting policies, services, or production quantities. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Reference boundary
[stderr] 
[stderr] The functional reference constrains high-level party-based turn-based adventure
[stderr] scope only. It is never permission to reproduce branding, names, copy, story,
[stderr] characters, lore, assets, music, layouts, screen composition, maps, distinctive
[stderr] menus, flows, or interaction choreography. Original mechanics expression,
[stderr] content, UI, art, and feedback must be created for this product.
[stderr] # Generated projects
[stderr] 
[stderr] Each `supervisor initial` run creates one named workspace here and writes its
[stderr] project-specific `INITIAL.md`. The factory is run with the normal Supervisor
[stderr] collection command; there is no special `generate-runbooks` command.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] supervisor-run --project <project-name>
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] Each project workspace contains:
[stderr] 
[stderr] - `PROJECT_BRIEF.md`: normalized source brief;
[stderr] - `INITIAL.md`: the user-created source brief supplied to `supervisor-run`;
[stderr] - `specification/`: canonical requirements, platform, experience, and domain
[stderr]   specifications;
[stderr] - `planning/`: the complete work checklist, dependency graph, and plan for
[stderr]   creating the R files in manageable batches;
[stderr] - `authoring-runbooks/`: generated B-series runbook-writing files. Each writes
[stderr]   no more than seven R-series files; B files write Markdown instructions, not
[stderr]   product code;
[stderr] - `runbooks/`: generated R-series files: detailed instructions for the actual
[stderr]   implementation and verification work;
[stderr] - `.supervisor/`: isolated run state for the generated collections.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The relationship is deliberately layered:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] projects/<project-name>/INITIAL.md
[stderr]   → repository F001–F016 factory files (with --project <project-name>)
[stderr]   → projects/<project-name>/authoring-runbooks/B....md
[stderr]   → projects/<project-name>/runbooks/R....md
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] The F-series files are reusable source factory instructions. They create the
[stderr] project folder, specification, complete work checklist, and first B-series
[stderr] files. A B-series file is a *runbook writer*: it takes one small part of that
[stderr] checklist and writes no more than seven R-series Markdown files. “Bounded” is
[stderr] only that size limit. It does not build the application. An R-series file is a
[stderr] *product-work instruction*: it contains the detailed steps and success evidence
[stderr] for one small piece of real product work.
[stderr] 
[stderr] In other words, the step that creates the R files is not an F-series file:
[stderr] `F014` creates the first B files, then running each B file creates its R files.
[stderr] 
[stderr] For a very large product, a B dispatcher file creates more B-series runbook
[stderr] writers for the remaining areas of the checklist. This is how the collection
[stderr] scales to hundreds or thousands of R-series files without oversized prompts or
[stderr] a separate manual run. The parent collection explicitly registers its generated
[stderr] child collections, so the originating `--run-all` invocation follows them
[stderr] automatically.
[stderr] 
[stderr] `supervisor-run --project <project-name>` is the normal way to start or resume
[stderr] one project. It reads that workspace's `INITIAL.md`, runs the factory source
[stderr] collection, follows only child collections belonging to that workspace, and
[stderr] stores factory task state in `projects/<project-name>/.supervisor/factory.sqlite3`.
[stderr] Accepted tasks are skipped on later invocations; the first unfinished task is
[stderr] resumed using its existing collection state.
[stderr] # Runbook Generator
[stderr] 
[stderr] This repository is a generic, Supervisor-driven factory for turning a concise
[stderr] product brief into a complete, dependency-ordered implementation plan. It is
[stderr] not tied to a particular app type, framework, agent, or delivery platform.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Given a brief for a game, desktop application, web product, operating system,
[stderr] developer tool, service, document system, or any other product, the factory
[stderr] first discovers the product domains and creates a canonical specification. It
[stderr] then builds a scalable collection of detailed implementation runbooks.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Install Supervisor
[stderr] 
[stderr] Install the global Supervisor CLI once. It requires Python 3.10 or newer but
[stderr] does not require administrator access:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jakowicz/supervisor/main/scripts/install.sh -o /tmp/supervisor-install.sh
[stderr] bash /tmp/supervisor-install.sh
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] If the installer says `~/.local/bin` is not on your `PATH`, add the line it
[stderr] prints to your shell profile, then open a new terminal. Confirm the commands
[stderr] are available:
[stderr] 
